soc: sunxi: cpu_comm: hand the MIPS its call slot back after a callback

Every MipsHalCallback_* the firmware sends us cost one call slot for the
life of the boot, and at the 20th the firmware hung.

The receive path (command_action, entry_cmd <= 4) delivered the message to
the kernel handlers and to any open /dev/cpu_comm, then parked a reference
in the CallCmd ring at share_seq + 32 for a channel reader -- the stock
ARM driver's ioctl 0xC0087F03, BG_Thread on the MIPS -- which copies the
entry and hands the slot back with Comm_ReleaseFreeCall(). This driver
has no such reader. So the ring grew by one per callback, the sender's
FreeCall pool shrank by one, and with 19 references the MIPS's
SendComm2CPUEx spun in fifo_isNearlyFull() before it even asked for a
slot: elog full of "cmdfifo.type=0, target cpu=0" (cpu_comm_core.c:473),
tick counter frozen, debug shell dead, every RPC -110.

Measured 08.09.2026 on the device: CallCmd(0,1,0) rd=0 wr=19, FreeCall
one slot free, watch counting 19 SignalChange callbacks -- one short of
the hang; the earlier post mortem had the same numbers after it. With
about three callbacks per resolution change that is the "state tips after
four or five changes" of doku/97 3b. The firmware side and the numbers:
doku/nachtlog/S10; the device run: S11.

So do what the reader would do: copy the entry, and after the ACK give
the slot back with Comm_ReleaseFreeCall() on the sender's share sequence
-- the call comm_CallWorkAction() already makes for the high-priority
class. A RETURN only where the sender waits for one, the same condition
as there; both known callbacks are NOTIFY and get none, the MIPS keeps no
wait object for them. Nothing goes into the CallCmd ring any more.

And make it visible: `watch` now shows the incoming pool too. With the
fix the ring must read "ungelesen 0" and the FreeCall side keep its 19 or
20 free slots across any number of callbacks; without it "ungelesen" grew
by one per callback.

--- a/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_proto.c	2026-09-08 00:57:18.853035543 +0200
+++ b/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_proto.c	2026-09-08 00:59:09.408969571 +0200
@@ -898,6 +898,8 @@
 	unsigned long entry_base;		/* virtual address of the call entry */
 	u16 component_id;
 	u32 session_id;
+	u8 local_msg[COMM_MSG_SIZE] __aligned(8);
+	bool release_call = false;
 
 	if (WARN_ON(remote_cpu > 1))
 		return;
@@ -996,13 +998,35 @@
 		 * so a handler sees both message classes exactly once. */
 		cpu_comm_kernel_deliver((const void *)entry_base);
 
-		if (entry_cmd <= 4)
-			Comm_Add2NewCallFifo(
-				(void *)((u8 *)pcpu_comm_dev + 48),
-				(u32 *)(share_seq_r + 32),
-				entry_base);
-		else
+		if (entry_cmd <= 4) {
+			/*
+			 * Stock parks a reference to the entry in the CallCmd ring
+			 * at share_seq_r + 32 here and wakes the channel; a reader
+			 * picks it up later, copies it, hands the slot back with
+			 * Comm_ReleaseFreeCall() and only then the sender's pool
+			 * has it again. That reader is the channel ioctl of the
+			 * stock ARM driver (0xC0087F03) and BG_Thread on the MIPS.
+			 * This driver has neither: delivery above works on copies,
+			 * nobody drains the ring and nobody releases the slot.
+			 *
+			 * The cost was one slot per callback, for the life of the
+			 * boot. With 19 references in the ring the MIPS sender
+			 * spins in fifo_isNearlyFull() before it even asks for a
+			 * slot (SendComm2CPUEx, cpu_comm_core.c:473) and the
+			 * firmware is gone: no callback, no RPC answer, no shell.
+			 * Measured 08.09.2026 -- ring rd=0 wr=19, one free slot,
+			 * exactly at the 20th SignalChange; on the wall it was the
+			 * "state tips after four or five resolution changes"
+			 * (doku/nachtlog/S10, S11).
+			 *
+			 * So do what the reader would do: copy, and release after
+			 * the ACK below. Nothing goes into the ring any more.
+			 */
+			memcpy(local_msg, (const void *)entry_base, COMM_MSG_SIZE);
+			release_call = true;
+		} else {
 			Comm_Add2Call2WQ((void *)entry_base);
+		}
 
 		/* Increment incoming call counter */
 		if (pcpu_comm_dev)
@@ -1087,6 +1111,34 @@
 		   *(u8 *)(share_seq_r + 8),
 		   *(u32 *)(share_seq_r + 20),
 		   *(u32 *)(share_seq_r + 24));
+
+	if (release_call) {
+		/*
+		 * The receiver's duty: the slot goes back into the sender's
+		 * pool. share_seq_r is getShareSeqR(remote_cpu, 0), whose local
+		 * cpu is this one, so Comm_ReleaseFreeCall()'s owner check lets
+		 * it through -- the same call comm_CallWorkAction() makes for the
+		 * high-priority class. After the ACK, as in stock, where the
+		 * release comes from the reader thread; the sender holds its
+		 * send semaphore until the ACK, so nobody else touches the pool
+		 * in between.
+		 */
+		Comm_ReleaseFreeCall((void *)share_seq_r, (void *)entry_base);
+
+		/*
+		 * A RETURN only if the sender waits for one -- the condition
+		 * comm_CallWorkAction() uses. Both known callbacks are NOTIFY
+		 * (+6 = 0x0001): the MIPS keeps no wait object for them and
+		 * asserts in its command_action if one is answered.
+		 */
+		if (!(local_msg[6] & MSG_FLAG_NOTIFY) ||
+		    (local_msg[6] & MSG_FLAG_RETURN_ACK)) {
+			*(u16 *)(local_msg + 8) = 0;	/* no return values */
+			if (SendComm2CPUEx((unsigned long)local_msg, remote_cpu, 0))
+				pr_warn_ratelimited("cpu_comm: command_action: RETURN fuer comp=0x%08x fehlgeschlagen\n",
+						    *(u32 *)(local_msg + 40));
+		}
+	}
 }
 
